<div class="page">
  <h2 style="font-size: 14pt; margin-top: 0;">How To Send In Your Payment</h2>
  <p style="font-size: 10pt;">When sending a payment with Form 4868, use the addresses in the middle column under Where To File on page 2.</p>
  <ul style="font-size: 10pt; margin-top: 5px;">
    <li>Make your check or money order payable to "Internal Revenue Service" (not "IRS"). Do not send cash.</li>
    <li>Write your social security number, daytime phone number, and "1995 Form 4868" on your check or money order.</li>
    <li>Do not staple or attach your payment to the form.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 style="font-size: 14pt; margin-top: 20px;">If You Need Additional Time</h2>
  <p style="font-size: 10pt;">If the automatic extension does not give you enough time, you can ask for additional time. But you'll have to give a good reason, and it must be approved by the IRS. You must file the additional time request before the other of the following:</p>
  <ul style="font-size: 10pt; margin-top: 5px;">
    <li>File Form 2888, Application for Additional Extension of Time to File U.S. Individual Income Tax Return, or</li>
    <li>File Form 4868 before you file Form 2888 or write a letter asking for more time. Only in cases of undue hardship will the IRS approve your request for an extension.</li>
  </ul>
  <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Ask early for this extra time. Then, you can still file your return on time if your request is not approved.</p>

  <h2 style="font-size: 14pt; margin-top: 20px;">Filing Your Tax Return</h2>
  <p style="font-size: 10pt;">You may file your tax return any time before the extension expires. But remember, Form 4868 does not extend the time to pay taxes. If you do not pay the amount due by the regular due date, you will owe interest. You may also be charged penalties.</p>
  <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Do not attach a copy of Form 4868 to your return.</p>

  <p style="font-size: 10pt;">You will owe interest on any tax not paid by the regular due date of your return. The interest runs until you pay the tax. Even if you had a good reason for not paying on time, you will still owe interest.</p>

  <h2 style="font-size: 14pt; margin-top: 20px;">Late Payment Penalty</h2>
  <p style="font-size: 10pt;">The penalty is usually <span data-field="penalty_rate">{{ penalty_rate }}</span> of any tax (other than estimated tax) not paid by the regular due date. It is charged for each month or part of the month tax is unpaid. The maximum penalty is <span data-field="max_penalty_percent">{{ max_penalty_percent }}</span>.</p>

  <p style="font-size: 10pt;">The late payment penalty will not be charged if you attach a statement to your return explaining the reason. Do not attach the statement to Form 4868.</p>
  <p style="font-size: 10pt;">You are considered to have "reasonable cause" for the late payment if you have a good reason for not paying before the regular due date of your return through withholding, estimated tax payments, or with Form 4868.</p>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <h2 style="font-size: 14pt; margin-top: 0;">Specific Instructions</h2>
  <p style="font-size: 10pt;"><strong>Box 1</strong></p>
  <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Enter your name(s) and address. If you plan to file a return, include both spouses' names in the order in which they will appear on the return.</p>
  <p style="font-size: 10pt;">If you want correspondence regarding this extension to be sent to you at an address other than your own or your spouse's, enter that address in Box 1.</p>
  <p style="font-size: 10pt;">If you changed your mailing address after you filed your last return because of marriage, divorce, etc., be sure to report this to your Social Security Administration before filing Form 4868. This prevents delays in processing your extension request.</p>

  <p style="font-size: 10pt;"><strong>Line 2</strong></p>
  <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Enter the last 6 digits of your social security number.</p>

  <p style="font-size: 10pt;"><strong>Lines 3 and 4</strong></p>
  <p style="font-size: 10pt;">If you plan to file jointly, enter the SSN on line 3 that corresponds to the primary taxpayer, and the SSN on line 4.</p>

  <p style="font-size: 10pt;"><strong>Line 6a—Total Tax Liability for 1995</strong></p>
  <p style="font-size: 10pt;">This is the amount you expect to enter on Form 1040, line 10; Form 1040A, line 28; Form 1040, line 54; or Form 1040-T, line 31. If you expect this amount to be zero, enter zero.</p>
  <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Caution: You can estimate this amount, but be as exact as you can with the information you have. If we later find that your estimate was not reasonable, the extension will be null and void.</p>

  <p style="font-size: 10pt;"><strong>Line 6b—Total Payment for 1995</strong></p>
  <p style="font-size: 10pt;">This is the amount you expect to enter on Form 1040EZ, line 9; Form 1040A, line 29d; Form 1040, line 61 (excluding line 58); or Form 1040-T, line 37.</p>

